.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt DSP 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dsp
.Nd OSS's audio device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/soundcard.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dsp
.Pa /dev/dsp1
.Pa /dev/adsp1
.Pa /dev/dsp2
.Pa /dev/dsp3
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The device plays the samples a program writes to it and records the samples
a program reads from it, through OSS's interface with Linux's numbers: major
14, minor 3.
It is there only while the audio driver in use has streams.
The node is mode 0666, so that the console's user plays sound.
.Pp
Any number of descriptors play and record at once.
An open for writing plays, one for reading records, and one for both does
both at once, full duplex, with one format, channel count, rate and fragment
size for the two; on a device of one direction, one for both takes that one
alone.
An open sets OSS's defaults, unsigned 8-bit mono at 8000 Hz, and each has
its own settings, buffer and delay.
.Pp
The kernel mixes what every descriptor plays into one stream of the device:
stereo at 48000 Hz, of 24 bits on an HD Audio codec that has them and of
16 bits on one that has not, or 16 bits at 44100 Hz on the Sound Blaster 16.
Each descriptor's samples are converted to 24-bit stereo, and to that rate by
a windowed-sinc filter, as FreeBSD's kernel converts them: what lies below 0.9
of the lower rate's Nyquist frequency is kept within 0.1 dB, and what lies
above it is attenuated by at least 80 dB, so that a rate converted down does
not alias and one converted up has no images.
The filter delays a converted descriptor by 59 frames of the lower of its
rate and the mix's, 1.2 ms against 48000 Hz and 7.4 ms at 8000 Hz, which its
delay and position count.
At the mix's own rate a descriptor's samples reach the mix unchanged.
The sum is held within 24 bits and, on a device of 16 bits, rounded to 16.
The stream line of
.Pa /proc/audio
.Pq Xr proc 5
gives the bits HD Audio plays.
A descriptor that starts while others play joins the mix after what the
driver's buffer already holds.
When a descriptor stops, by
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_RESET ,
a trigger turned off or a nonblocking close, what the mix took from it and
the device has not played is not taken back: it plays out with the others,
or is dropped when no other descriptor plays.
.Pp
What is written is queued in a buffer of periods, the fragments of OSS.
Without
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT
a period is the power of two of bytes nearest a 43rd of a second, about
23 ms, at least 128 bytes, and the buffer has four of them.
The device starts playing once enough is queued to fill the periods of the
driver's own buffer, which the buffer holds with at least one period more,
or at
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_POST ,
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SYNC
or the last
.Xr close 2 .
When the program falls behind, the device plays silence and goes on.
.Pp
.Xr write 2
takes whole or partial frames; a partial frame waits for the write that
completes it.
While the buffer is full a write waits for room.
A signal ends the wait: the write returns the bytes it took, or fails with
.Er EINTR
when it took none.
With
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
the write takes what fits and fails with
.Er EAGAIN
when nothing does.
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLOUT
while a whole period of the buffer is free.
The last close waits for what is queued to play, unless the descriptor is
nonblocking or a signal comes, and then stops the device.
.Pp
Recording starts at the first
.Xr read 2 ,
or at a
.Xr poll 2
for
.Dv POLLIN ,
and goes on until the last close,
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_RESET ,
or a
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETTRIGGER
that turns input off.
What the device records is kept in a buffer of periods laid out as
playback's, which a read empties.
A read waits until it has every byte it asked for, and may end within a
frame; the next read goes on from there.
A signal ends the wait: the read returns the bytes it took, or fails with
.Er EINTR
when it took none.
With
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
the read takes what there is and fails with
.Er EAGAIN
when there is nothing.
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN
while a whole period has been recorded.
When the program falls behind by the whole buffer, what the device records
next is dropped until a read makes room, so the program reads the oldest
frames in order and then a gap.
The close drops what was recorded and not read.
.Pp
A change of format, channels, rate or fragments stops the device and empties
the buffers.
A request that only asks, a rate or channel count of 0, or
.Dv AFMT_QUERY
or a format the device lacks, changes nothing and empties nothing.
The requests:
.Bl -tag -width SNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETFMT , SNDCTL_DSP_SAMPLESIZE
Set one of
.Dv AFMT_U8 ,
.Dv AFMT_S16_LE ,
.Dv AFMT_S24_LE
(0x8000, signed 24 bits in the low three bytes of four),
.Dv AFMT_S24_PACKED
(0x40000, three bytes) and
.Dv AFMT_S32_LE
(0x1000), and give back the format in use; another value, a mask of several, or
.Dv AFMT_QUERY ,
leaves the format as it is.
The three wider formats have the numbers of OSS 4, which
.In linux/soundcard.h
lacks; a program defines them itself.
A played sample keeps its top 24 bits, rounded to 16 on a device of 16
bits; a recorded one has the 16 bits the device records on top of zeros.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETFMTS
The five formats
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETFMT
takes.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_CHANNELS
Set the count asked for, held at two on
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
and give back the count in use; 0 asks for it.
The HDMI devices grant up to 128, as Linux's OSS layer does, and more than
128 fails.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_STEREO
Set stereo for a nonzero value and mono for 0, and give back 1 or 0.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SPEED
Set the rate nearest the one asked for, between 1000 and 192000 Hz and within
what the device plays or records, and give back the rate set; 0 asks for it.
Playback takes any rate up to four times the mix's, which it converts to the
mix's.
HDA records at the rate its converter has nearest the one asked for, and
converts none.
In an open for both, the rate given back is playback's, and recording's may
differ where the device's converter has other rates.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT
Ask for periods of 2 to the power of the low 16 bits bytes and for the
number of them in the high 16 bits.
A period is from 128 to 16384 bytes, and holds a frame at least: one asked
smaller is the smallest power of two that does.
There are at least two and as many as 64 KiB hold, or one more for playback.
A playback period is at most the frames, at the rate set, that one period of
the mix plays: 1024 frames of the mix on HD Audio, and 512 on the Sound
Blaster 16.
A playback buffer holds the periods the mix takes at its start and at least
one more, so that a program that keeps a period free, as cmus does, can
queue the start.
On a device of its own whose buffer holds more of the stream than that,
it grows to hold them and a period more, up to 8 MiB.
A recording period may be made smaller by the driver.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETBLKSIZE
The bytes of a period.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETOSPACE
An
.Vt audio_buf_info :
the whole periods free, the periods of the buffer, the bytes of a period and
the bytes free.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETISPACE
An
.Vt audio_buf_info :
the whole periods recorded and not read, the periods of the buffer, the bytes
of a period and the bytes ready to read.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETODELAY
The bytes queued ahead of the next one written, in the buffer and in the
mix: how long, at the rate set, before what is written next is heard.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETOPTR
A
.Vt count_info :
the bytes played since the open, silence among them, wrapping; the periods
played since the last
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETOPTR ;
and the play position's offset in the buffer.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETIPTR
The same for recording: the bytes recorded since the open, those dropped
among them, the periods recorded since the last
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETIPTR ,
and the record position's offset in the buffer.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETCAPS
.Dv DSP_CAP_REALTIME
and
.Dv DSP_CAP_TRIGGER ,
revision 1, and
.Dv DSP_CAP_DUPLEX
in an open for both.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETDUPLEX
Nothing, in an open for both, where the device is full duplex already.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETTRIGGER , SNDCTL_DSP_SETTRIGGER
.Dv PCM_ENABLE_OUTPUT
and
.Dv PCM_ENABLE_INPUT ,
each of the open's directions on at open.
Turning output off stops playback, dropping what the mix has taken and
keeping the rest; turning it on starts playback if anything is queued.
Turning input off stops recording, keeping what was recorded for the reads;
turning it on starts recording.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_POST
Start the device if anything is queued, however little.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SYNC
Start the device, wait for what is queued to play, then stop it and drop a
partial frame left over.
With the trigger off, or in an open for reading alone, it returns at once.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_RESET
Stop playback and recording at once and empty both buffers.
.It Dv SNDCTL_DSP_NONBLOCK
Set
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
on the descriptor.
.It Dv OSS_GETVERSION
0x030802, the version Linux gives.
.El
The first capture reader configures the stopped input.
With several readers open, its settings stay fixed and each reader converts
from them to its own format, channels and rate, through the same filter.
A slow reader loses only its own frames.
The last running reader stops the input, and the last close releases it.
.Pp
With the boot option
.Cm rach2.audio.loopback ,
capture reads the clipped playback mix before the hardware master volume.
A silent playback channel supplies the clock while a reader runs, including
on a Sound Blaster 16 without a hardware input.
.Ss HDMI
.Pa /dev/dsp1 ,
minor 19,
.Pa /dev/adsp1 ,
minor 28, and
.Pa /dev/dsp2 ,
minor 35, play on the monitors of the HDMI and DisplayPort outputs of
Haswell's Intel display, through the display's HDMI codec, as Linux's
second card's
.Pa dsp
and
.Pa adsp
do, each on its own PCM of the codec.
The codec has a PCM for each of its converters, three on Haswell, and
.Pa /dev/dsp3 ,
minor 51, plays on a fourth, where a codec has one.
Linux's OSS layer names only a card's first two PCMs; the others have
the names it gives the next cards' first, since Rach2 has no ALSA PCM
devices, through which Linux reaches them.
They are there while the codec is bound to the display driver,
.Xr i915 4 .
Each monitor whose ELD turns valid is given a PCM, the one it had last if
no other has taken it since, else the first free, as Linux gives them:
the first monitor plays on
.Pa /dev/dsp1 ,
a second on
.Pa /dev/adsp1
and a third on
.Pa /dev/dsp2 ,
all at once.
A device whose PCM has no monitor plays to none, and a monitor given its
PCM while it plays is given the stream.
Each takes the requests and formats of
.Pa /dev/dsp ,
but plays its PCM's one stream without the mix: one descriptor plays at a
time on each, and nothing records.
It grants each format, one to 128 channels and any rate from 1000 to
192000 Hz, and converts them to what the converter and the monitor's ELD
allow: the nearest of its rates from 32000 to 192000 Hz, in 16 bits, or
24 when the monitor takes them and the format is wider than 16, and the
even count of channels from two to the most a descriptor of the ELD has,
eight at most, nearest the count asked for, the higher on a tie.
It keeps the rate asked for, and its buffer holds 40 ms of the converted
stream: for 128 channels of 32 bits at 192000 Hz, some 3.9 MiB, which the
open's buffer grows to hold.
A mono stream plays on every channel, and another's channels play in
order, those the device lacks dropped and those the stream lacks silent;
the rate is changed by linear interpolation, as Linux's OSS plugins change
it.
More than two channels are sent with the CEA-861 channel allocation the
monitor's speakers allow, in Linux's order of preference.
An open for both directions plays alone.
When the display driver lets the codec go, a write or read in progress
returns what it moved,
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SYNC
fails, and every later call but the close fails with
.Er ENODEV ,
as on Linux.
.Pa /proc/asound
has the card and each monitor's ELD, as
.Xr proc 5
says.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBUSY
.Xr open 2 :
another kernel caller has taken the hardware stream directly.
The console's audio calls share the mix and do not prevent an open.
On an HDMI device, another descriptor plays the device, or every converter
of the codec is taken.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
.Xr open 2 :
the driver has no streams, or none for the direction an open for reading or
writing asks for, or neither direction for an open for both: the Sound
Blaster 16's driver and the HDMI devices record nothing.
Any call but
.Xr close 2 :
the device has gone, as when the display driver lets the HDMI devices'
codec go;
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLERR
and
.Dv POLLNVAL .
.It Bq Er EBADF
A write to an open for reading alone, or a read from one for writing alone.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal ended a write or a read that took nothing, or the wait of
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SYNC ,
which leaves the device playing.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking write found no room, or a nonblocking read nothing recorded.
.It Bq Er EIO
The device failed to start.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
.Xr open 2 :
no memory for the buffers.
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETFMT ,
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_CHANNELS ,
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SPEED
or
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETFRAGMENT :
no memory for the larger playback buffer an HDMI device needs for the
settings; the old ones stay.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Xr open 2
with an access mode of 3; a request for a direction the open does not have,
such as
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_GETISPACE
in an open for writing alone, or
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_SETDUPLEX
in an open of one direction;
.Dv SNDCTL_DSP_CHANNELS
of more than 128; another of OSS's requests.
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
Another request.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr audiotest 1 ,
.Xr i915 4 ,
.Xr mixer 4 ,
.Xr proc 5
